title: '? Containing an overrider should count as integrating the original it stands for. Not built.'
pid: 67f2e38a-1099-4357-a521-f2ca38b3a3ca
body: |2-
  The unintegrated subtraction still compares IDs only. After placing
  the fork and saving, a running view still drew the subscribee with
  'C2/', the original among the two, though the subscriber now held
  its fork; only the removal that followed made it 'C1/2'. Making the
  subtraction use 'member_it_stands_for' too would end that, but it
  touches every render and the stats, so it waits for Jeff's word.
  Top asked on 2026-11-14 what I would build, what it costs and what it
  breaks, to put one question to Jeff. The question: "When your note
  holds someone's reply to one of its messages, should that message stop
  showing as new?" My answer is yes, with "not built" as the default
  if he does not answer.
  What I would build: in 'less_what_the_subscriber_integrates'
  ('server/refresh/goal.rs'), count as contained, besides each ID the
  subscriber contains, every node that ID overrides, walking anyone's
  override records the skgrepo restriction shows, as the hide inference
  does. The stats count from the same function, so they follow. Then
  restore the symmetry I took out: placing an overrider would drop the
  hide of its original, since containing it now integrates it.
  What it costs: one walk of the override records per contained ID, per
  subscribee as such drawn and per stats pass. Override chains are one
  or two links long, so this is small; about thirty lines and two tests.
  What it breaks: nothing a user relies on, but three things change.
  The test 'test_placing_an_overrider_of_a_hidden_node_keeps_its_hide'
  and the paragraph of 'docs/forks.org' saying containing X' does not
  integrate X would be rewritten. Holding anyone's fork of a member would
  silence the member, even a fork made for another purpose than a reply;
  I think that is right, since one holds a version of it. And it does not
  reach what Top also asks about, what Jeff later writes under his reply:
  that is below the first layer of the subscription, which no rule of
  this kind can see; it is the design of [[id:d3ed5d37-2a37-441a-b82b-03a39a89fdf2][reporting changes at any depth]].
  Since 2026-11-14 the reader's own side no longer needs this: a reply
  typed in the unread list files the original by its own ID ("Writing in an unintegrated
  child, where the subscribee as such draws it, integrates it."). The question remains for a sender who places the
  reply's own node, as Top did with Jeff's.
